│       banneton           ││                                                                      │
│       dutch-oven         ││   Flour, water and time                                              │
│                          ││                                                                      │
│                          ││   Wheat flour carries two storage proteins, glutenin and gliadin.    │
│                          ││   When water is added and the flour rests, they unwind and begin to  │
│                          ││   bond into an elastic network. Given time and gentle handling that  │
│                          ││   network develops on its own, which is why a long rest can replace  │
│                          ││   much of the hard kneading a recipe asks for.                       │
│                          ││                                                                      │
│                          ││   Enzymes in the flour, mainly amylase, slowly cut starches into     │
│                          ││   sugars. Those sugars feed the yeast and, later, sweeten the crust  │
│                          ││   through browning. A dough that rests longer tastes faintly sweeter │
│                          ││   and browns deeper for exactly this reason.                         │
│                          ││                                                                      │
│                          ││   Gas, structure and heat                                            │
